        <div id="compressor-vs-thermoelectric" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Compressor Fridge vs Thermoelectric Cooler
          </h2>

          <p>
            Search for an electric cooler for camping and you will find two very different technologies sold side by side, often with similar photos. A compressor fridge works the same way as your kitchen refrigerator: it compresses refrigerant, which carries heat out of the box. A thermoelectric cooler uses a solid-state Peltier module that moves heat when current flows through it. The first is a real refrigerator. The second is closer to a cooler with a fan.
          </p>

          <p>
            The difference shows up on a hot day. A compressor fridge holds whatever temperature you set, typically anywhere from about 50F down to 0F or below, regardless of whether it is 70F or 100F outside. A thermoelectric cooler can only cool to roughly 36F to 40F below the surrounding air. In a 95F car, that leaves food at 55F or more, well above the 40F food-safety threshold for perishables.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm"></th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Compressor fridge</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Thermoelectric cooler</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Temperature control</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Holds a set temperature, can freeze</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Fixed amount below ambient</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Hot-day performance</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Holds 38F at 100F outside</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cannot reach safe food temperatures</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Power use</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cycles on and off, 250-500Wh/day typical</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Runs constantly, 1,000-1,400Wh/day</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Battery protection</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Adjustable low-voltage cutoff on most</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often none</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Price</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $200 to $1,000+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About $80 to $200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Best for</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Camping, overlanding, vans, RVs</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Drinks on a road trip</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Power use is the other surprise. A compressor fridge cycles on and off once it reaches temperature, often running only a third of the time in mild weather. A thermoelectric cooler draws 4 to 5 amps continuously, every hour it is plugged in. That means a thermoelectric cooler typically uses three to four times more energy per day than a compressor fridge while keeping food much warmer. It is a bad match for any battery or solar setup.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is a place for thermoelectric coolers: keeping drinks and pre-chilled snacks cool on a long drive, with the engine running. For camping, where food has to stay safe for days and power comes from a battery, buy a compressor fridge. The price gap has narrowed enough that a small compressor fridge now costs about the same as a premium hard cooler, and it will never need ice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-draw" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. How Much Power a 12V Fridge Uses Per Day
          </h2>

          <p>
            Ignore the rated wattage on the label when estimating battery life. A 45-liter compressor fridge might be rated at 45 to 60 watts, but that is only what it draws while the compressor runs. Once the box reaches temperature, the compressor cycles on and off, and the fraction of time it runs, called the duty cycle, decides how much energy it uses per day.
          </p>

          <p>
            Duty cycle depends on outside temperature, how cold you set the fridge, how often you open the lid and how full the box is. In mild weather with the fridge at 38F, a good compressor fridge may run 20% to 30% of the time. On a 95F day in a closed car, it may run 50% or more. Dometic rates the CFX3 45 at about 1.03Ah per hour in 90F heat, which is roughly 25Ah or 300Wh per day.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Typical Daily Energy, 45L Fridge at 38F</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">200-300Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Mild weather, 70F</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">300-450Wh</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Hot weather, 90F</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">500Wh+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Freezer mode or dual zone</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">1,000Wh+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Thermoelectric cooler</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A practical planning number for a single-zone 45-liter compressor fridge is 300 to 450Wh per day in summer, or 25 to 35Ah at 12V. Small 20-liter fridges use less, and large or dual-zone fridges running a freezer side use more. If you set the whole box to freezer temperatures, expect energy use to climb sharply, because the compressor is fighting a much larger temperature difference.
          </p>

          <p>
            The easiest way to cut power use is to start cold. Pre-chill the fridge on household power the night before a trip, and load it with food that is already cold or frozen. Pulling warm drinks down to 38F is the most energy-intensive thing a fridge does. Keep it full, since a full fridge holds its temperature better than an empty one, and open the lid only when you need to.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="power-station" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Running a 12V Fridge on a Portable Power Station
          </h2>

          <p>
            A portable power station is the easiest way to run a 12V camper refrigerator away from the vehicle. The math is straightforward: take the station's rated capacity, multiply by about 0.85 to account for conversion losses, and divide by the fridge's daily energy use. A 1,024Wh station gives roughly 870Wh of usable energy, which runs a 45-liter compressor fridge using 350Wh per day for about two and a half days.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
