                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 15-17 hours</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 4-5 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">500-700Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">425-600Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.2-1.7 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 8-12 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">1,000-1,100Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">850-935Wh</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 2.5 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 17-19 hours</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">2,000Wh+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,700Wh+</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 5 days</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 1.4 days</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Plug the fridge into the station's 12V car socket, not an AC outlet. Running the fridge through the station's AC inverter and then through the fridge's own AC adapter wastes 10% to 20% of the energy as heat on the way, and leaves the inverter running all day with its own idle draw. Every fridge on this list has a 12V DC cord, and every mid-size power station has a 12V output.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check one detail: some power stations turn off their 12V output after a period of low draw, or their 12V socket is limited to around 10A. A compressor fridge draws a few amps and cycles on and off, which usually works fine, but confirm that your station's 12V output stays on continuously. Our explainer on whether <a href="/can-a-portable-power-station-run-a-refrigerator/">a power station can run a refrigerator</a> covers the full math, including household fridges.
          </p>

          <p>
            For weekend car camping, a station in the 500 to 1,000Wh range is the sweet spot: enough for a fridge, phones and lights for two nights, light enough to carry. Our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-camping/">best portable power stations for camping</a> ranks the best options by weight and capacity, and our guide to <a href="/what-size-portable-power-station-do-i-need/">what size power station you need</a> walks through sizing for everything else you plan to run.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="solar" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Solar Fridge and Solar Powered Cooler Setups
          </h2>

          <p>
            A solar powered fridge is almost always a regular 12V compressor fridge connected to a solar panel through a battery. A few models, like the BougeRV CR45 and the EcoFlow GLACIER Classic, accept solar input directly, but even those need a battery to run through the night. A fridge fed by a panel alone would stop at sunset, and while good insulation keeps things cold for a few hours, it will not carry food safely through a warm night.
          </p>

          <p>
            The good news is that a fridge is one of the easiest loads to run on solar. A 100W panel in good summer sun typically produces somewhere around 350 to 500Wh per day after real-world losses, which is close to what a 45-liter fridge uses in hot weather. A 200W panel gives a comfortable margin and covers cloudy days. Pair that with a battery of at least 500Wh, or a 100Ah lithium house battery, and you can run a fridge indefinitely in summer.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SOLAR FRIDGE RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Size the panel for the fridge, and the battery for the night.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Roughly 100W to 200W of panel per compressor fridge in summer, and enough battery to cover at least one night and one cloudy day. Panels sold as solar powered coolers with no battery are a gimmick for anything perishable.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Folding portable panels work well for campers because you can park in the shade and set the panel in the sun, which keeps both the fridge and the car cooler. Rigid roof panels are better for vans and RVs because they charge whenever you drive or park. Our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-solar-panel/">best portable solar panels</a> covers the best folding options, and our explainer on <a href="/how-to-charge-a-portable-power-station-with-solar/">charging a power station with solar</a> explains voltage and connector compatibility.
          </p>

          <p>
            Winter is harder. Short days and low sun angles can cut solar output in half or more, though the fridge also uses less energy when it is cold outside. If you camp in the shoulder seasons, plan on a larger battery or a backup charging method, such as a DC-DC charger from the vehicle alternator or a small generator. For RV owners, our guide to the <a href="/best-rv-generator/">best RV generators</a> covers quiet options for topping up batteries on cloudy stretches.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="dual-zone" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Dual-Zone Fridges and Choosing a Size
          </h2>

          <p>
            A dual-zone fridge has two compartments with independent temperature settings, so one side can freeze while the other refrigerates. Some, like the EcoFlow GLACIER Classic 45L and 55L, use a removable divider, so you can run the box as one large fridge or split it. True dual-zone fridges use one compressor to manage both sides, with separate controls for each.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dual zone is worth it for trips of four days or more, when you want to freeze meat or meals in advance and thaw them one at a time, or keep ice cream and ice. For weekend trips, a single-zone fridge set to 36F to 38F is simpler, more efficient and gives you more usable space. Remember that running one side as a freezer adds considerably to daily energy use, so a dual-zone fridge needs a larger battery or more solar.
          </p>

          <p>
            For sizing, think in days and people. A 20-liter fridge holds a day or two of food for one or two people. A 35 to 45-liter fridge covers a long weekend for two or three people, which is why it is the most popular size. A 55-liter fridge suits a family of four for a weekend or a couple for a week. Anything larger, like the 75-liter dual-zone models, makes sense mostly for RVs and vans with permanent mounting.
          </p>

          <p>
            Measure your vehicle before buying. A 45-liter fridge is often 28 inches long, 18 inches wide and 20 inches tall, and it needs a few inches of clearance around the vents. Check that the lid can open fully where you plan to place it, and that you can lift it when full. Fridge slides that pull the unit out of the vehicle are a worthwhile upgrade for overlanders, especially for heavier models.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="low-voltage-cutoff" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Low-Voltage Cutoff and Battery Protection
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every good 12V fridge includes a low-voltage cutoff, often called battery protection. It monitors the battery voltage and shuts the fridge off before the battery drops too low. This is what keeps a fridge plugged into your car's socket overnight from leaving you with a vehicle that will not start in the morning.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most fridges offer three settings: high, medium and low. High shuts off earliest and is the right choice when the fridge runs from a vehicle's starter battery, because it leaves plenty of charge to crank the engine. Medium suits a vehicle with a modest auxiliary battery. Low lets the fridge draw a battery down furthest and is meant for a dedicated house battery, a lithium battery or a power station, where there is no engine to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Lithium batteries complicate this. LiFePO4 voltage stays nearly flat until the battery is almost empty, so a high or medium setting designed for lead-acid can cut the fridge off while a lithium battery still has plenty left, or a low setting may not trigger until the lithium battery's own BMS shuts down. For a lithium house battery or power station, set the fridge to low and let the battery's own protection handle the rest. Our guide to the <a href="/best-lifepo4-rv-battery/">best LiFePO4 RV batteries</a> explains how a lithium BMS protects the cells.
          </p>

          <p>
            Thermoelectric coolers are the weak point here. Many cheap plug-in car coolers have no low-voltage cutoff at all, so they will run a starter battery flat overnight. If a vehicle socket stays live with the ignition off, unplug a thermoelectric cooler when you park, or use a fridge with real battery protection.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. Placement, Ventilation and Care
          </h2>

          <p>
            A compressor fridge needs air. The compressor dumps heat out of vents, usually on one end or the back, and if those vents are blocked by luggage or pressed against a wall, the fridge runs longer, uses more power and may not reach temperature. Leave at least two to four inches of clearance around the vents, and avoid packing sleeping bags or bags of clothing around the fridge in a closed trunk.
          </p>

          <p>
            Keep it out of direct sun when you can. A fridge sitting in a hot, closed car on a summer day works hard all afternoon. Parking in the shade, cracking a window or using a reflective cover can cut energy use noticeably. At camp, place it in shade on level ground; most compressor fridges tolerate some tilt during driving but run best level.
          </p>

          <p>
            Wiring matters more than most owners realize. A cigarette-lighter socket and thin wiring can drop voltage enough to trigger the low-voltage cutoff early, especially on long runs to the back of an SUV or van. A dedicated 12V outlet wired with heavier cable directly to the battery, fused correctly, is a common and worthwhile upgrade for anyone who runs a fridge often.
          </p>

          <p>
            Between trips, clean the fridge, dry it fully and store it with the lid cracked open to prevent mildew and odors. Check the cord and plug for damage, since a loose 12V plug is the most common cause of a fridge that switches off by itself. A compressor fridge treated this way lasts many years, which makes the higher price easier to justify than a season of buying ice.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying a thermoelectric cooler for camping</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plug-in thermoelectric coolers only cool to roughly 36F to 40F below ambient. On a hot day, that leaves food above the 40F safety threshold. They also draw power constantly. For anything perishable or for multi-day trips, spend the extra on a compressor fridge, even a small budget one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running the fridge through an AC inverter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plugging a 12V fridge into a power station's AC outlet runs power through two conversions, wasting 10% to 20% of the energy as heat and keeping the inverter on all day. Use the station's 12V car socket and the fridge's DC cord instead, and the same battery lasts noticeably longer.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Loading it with warm food</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pulling warm drinks and groceries down to 38F is the most energy-intensive job a fridge does. Pre-chill the fridge on household power the night before, and load it with food that is already cold or frozen. It saves battery and gets food to a safe temperature faster.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using the wrong battery protection setting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The high setting protects a starter battery but can shut the fridge off early on a lithium battery or power station. The low setting on a starter battery can leave you unable to start the car. Match the setting to the battery you are actually using before every trip.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Blocking the vents</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A compressor fridge needs airflow to shed heat. Packing gear tight around the vents in a closed trunk makes it run longer, use more power and struggle to hold temperature. Leave a few inches of clearance on the vent side, and keep the fridge out of direct sun where possible.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best 12 volt camper refrigerator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Dometic CFX3 45 is the best overall for efficiency, build quality and battery protection. The ICECO VL45ProS offers most of that performance with a SECOP compressor for less. The BougeRV CR45 is the best budget compressor fridge for weekend campers. All three are true compressor fridges that hold safe food temperatures in hot weather.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a 12V fridge run on a power station?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Divide the station's usable capacity, about 85% of its rating, by the fridge's daily energy use. A 1,024Wh station has roughly 870Wh usable, and a 45-liter compressor fridge uses about 300 to 450Wh per day in summer, so expect about two to three days. A thermoelectric cooler would last less than one day on the same station.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can a solar panel run a fridge directly?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Not reliably on its own. A fridge connected only to a panel stops at night and slows down in clouds. Run the panel into a battery or power station and the fridge from that. Roughly 100W to 200W of panel per compressor fridge in summer, plus enough battery for a night and a cloudy day, keeps it running indefinitely.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a 12V fridge drain my car battery?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A compressor fridge with battery protection set to high will shut itself off before it drains your starter battery, leaving enough charge to start the engine. A cheap thermoelectric cooler often has no cutoff and can drain a battery overnight. For extended camping, use an auxiliary battery or power station rather than the starter battery.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a dual-zone fridge worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For trips of four days or longer, yes, because you can freeze meat or meals in one compartment while keeping fresh food in the other. For weekends, a single-zone fridge is simpler, more efficient and gives more usable space. Running a freezer zone uses considerably more power, so size your battery and solar accordingly.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What size camping fridge do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 20-liter fridge suits one or two people for a day or two. A 35 to 45-liter fridge covers a long weekend for two or three people and is the most popular size. A 55-liter fridge fits a family of four for a weekend. Larger 75-liter models make sense mainly for vans and RVs with permanent mounting.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are cheap 12V fridges worth buying?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Budget compressor fridges from BougeRV and Alpicool are worth buying for occasional campers; they cool properly and cost a fraction of premium models. Expect thinner insulation, more mixed long-term reliability and shorter warranties. Avoid the many no-name listings with no support. Cheap thermoelectric coolers are not worth buying for perishable food.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

